<code>assert 0 <= index && index <= \old(this.size()) && role != null => \forall int i; index <= i && i < \old(this.size()); (this.get(i) == null && \old(this.get(i)) == null) || this.get(i) == \old(this.get(i)) ;</code>
--------------------
<code>assert 0 <= index && index <= \old(this.size()) && role != null => \forall int i; index <= i && i < \old(this.size()); this.get(i).equals(\old(this.get(i))) || (this.get(i) == null && \old(this.get(i)) == null);</code>
--------------------
<code>assert 0 <= index && index <= \old(this.size()) && role != null => \forall int i; index <= i && i < \old(this.size()); this.get(i) == (\old(this.get(i)) == null ? null : \old(this.get(i)));</code>

This assert statement ensures that if the index is within the valid range (0 to the old size of the list inclusive) and the role is not null, then for every position from the index to the old size of the list, the element at the next position is either null (if the old element at that position was null) or it is equal to the old element at that position.
